WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:07.966
All right, stop right there.

00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:12.262
I have been asked about this
particular scene so many times,

00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:16.016
and they want to know how we
train to be able to do that.

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:17.100
We don't.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.102
All right, there is so much stuff

00:00:19.102 --> 00:00:20.895
that is incorrect in this.

00:00:20.895 --> 00:00:22.522
What's going on? My name is Andy Stumpf.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:26.234
I spent just under 17
years as a US Navy SEAL,

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:29.112
and I host the "Cleared Hot" podcast.

00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:30.113
Today, we're going to be taking a look

00:00:30.113 --> 00:00:32.574
at some underwater
action scenes from movies

00:00:32.574 --> 00:00:34.200
and seeing how real they really are.

00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:37.120
One last thing. When you hit the water,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.039
don't forget to cut away your chute.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:41.291
90% of people killed during the HALO jump

00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:43.918
got tangled up in their
chutes and drowned.

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:46.713
I've never been in an
underwater fight in my life

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:50.675
with anybody or thing
except for my own equipment.

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:52.510
My belts have tried to kill me.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.471
Magnets have tried to kill me. Hoses.

00:00:54.471 --> 00:00:56.264
Like, you end up fighting
all of your gear.

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:59.893
So that portion of the brief was legit.

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:01.686
I've never seen anybody

00:01:01.686 --> 00:01:04.355
give an audible brief on an airplane,

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:06.149
'cause it's so loud that you can't hear.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.910
I am not aware of any
real-world operations

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.954
that started with a
parachute jump of any kind

00:01:19.954 --> 00:01:21.915
that went directly into a dive.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:23.333
You're probably going to do a jump

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:26.002
where you're pushing boats
out of the aircraft as well.

00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:27.837
The boat lands under its own parachute,

00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:29.881
you land next to it,
you get into the boat.

00:01:29.881 --> 00:01:30.924
And you're probably
going to have to travel

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:33.927
a substantial distance
to actually do the dive.

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:36.221
Because otherwise you're
flying an airplane

00:01:36.221 --> 00:01:38.890
directly over the top of where
you're trying to be sneaky,

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.391
and that's not how you play

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:42.435
varsity-level hide-and-seek, right?

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:43.728
Cutting away from your parachute

00:01:43.728 --> 00:01:46.064
before you get into the water
is actually really dangerous,

00:01:46.064 --> 00:01:47.941
because at lower altitudes,

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.150
it's really hard to judge

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.653
how high you are up above the water.

00:01:51.653 --> 00:01:52.737
The rule that we use:

00:01:52.737 --> 00:01:54.280
Wait until your body hits the water

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.032
to cut away from your equipment.

00:01:56.032 --> 00:01:57.158
There have been people who cut away

00:01:57.158 --> 00:01:58.368
and they thought they were at 10 feet,

00:01:58.368 --> 00:02:00.662
but they were at 100 feet or 150 feet.

00:02:00.662 --> 00:02:02.205
And at some point,

00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:04.290
the surface tension of
water can be like concrete,

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.126
and [claps] you can kind of do the math.

00:02:09.712 --> 00:02:11.422
You don't have to use a flare.

00:02:11.422 --> 00:02:13.299
I mean, they make flashlights.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:14.509
They last longer.

00:02:14.509 --> 00:02:16.886
You don't have to worry
about getting burned.

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:27.272
People would actually probably
be a little bit underwhelmed

00:02:27.272 --> 00:02:29.858
with the type of diving that
you do in the SEAL teams.

00:02:29.858 --> 00:02:32.110
Not by the volume of diving,

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.864
but it's very focused
on getting from A to B.

00:02:35.864 --> 00:02:37.907
A lot of it is navigation

00:02:37.907 --> 00:02:40.910
to a place where you could
implant a charge or a sensor,

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:42.328
and then you're right back out.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:43.705
People who do this type of diving

00:02:43.705 --> 00:02:45.915
or the type of diving where
they're out on coral reefs,

00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:48.293
it's just a total different experience.

00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:51.337
Most of the diving that I did was at night

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.590
and at depths less than 20 feet.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:56.301
Not accurate at all. This is a zero.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.186
They actually did some really

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:09.022
robust breath-holding work for this movie.

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:11.900
And it's easy to get a long breath hold.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.526
Like, there are people
who can hold their breath

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:15.945
for, like, five, six,
seven, eight minutes.

00:03:15.945 --> 00:03:18.323
It's about the lowest
level of exertion, though.

00:03:18.323 --> 00:03:20.283
You don't see people holding their breath

00:03:20.283 --> 00:03:23.453
being incredibly active, which
is exactly what he was doing.

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:25.705
[choking]

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:35.965
The first introduction to
diving for SEAL candidates

00:03:35.965 --> 00:03:37.842
is open circuit in the pool.

00:03:37.842 --> 00:03:39.177
And I administered the test,

00:03:39.177 --> 00:03:41.262
and it would not be
uncommon to watch students

00:03:41.262 --> 00:03:42.639
have a shallow-water blackout

00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:44.891
or hold their breath until they pass out.

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:46.893
And one of the main indicators

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:49.062
that somebody is actually
about to become unconscious

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:51.022
is that bubbles will start
escaping their mouth,

00:03:51.022 --> 00:03:53.524
just like he simulated in the movie.

00:03:53.524 --> 00:03:55.568
So they actually obviously
put their time in

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:56.945
to research this.

00:03:56.945 --> 00:03:59.113
For the cinematic aspect

00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:01.532
and the actual likelihood
of this happening,

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:02.784
we're going to give them, like, a 3.

00:04:02.784 --> 00:04:05.662
And your muscles will stop
working in about 30 seconds.

00:04:05.662 --> 00:04:07.455
Nah, I'm sure it's not that fast.

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:09.707
Would you please listen for once?

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:13.920
So, this apparently is
occurring in the Arctic.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.131
His operational equipment consisted of

00:04:17.131 --> 00:04:19.342
a pair of board shorts
and what appeared to be

00:04:19.342 --> 00:04:21.386
a messenger bag slung over his shoulder,

00:04:21.386 --> 00:04:23.221
which is a controversial choice.

00:04:23.221 --> 00:04:24.973
You spend a good portion of your time

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:27.183
as a student in BUD/S hypothermic.

00:04:27.183 --> 00:04:28.977
I mean, we're talking
laying in the Pacific Ocean

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:29.936
down in San Diego.

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:32.063
Sometimes the water
temperature's in the 60s.

00:04:32.063 --> 00:04:33.564
We can get you hypothermic

00:04:33.564 --> 00:04:35.483
by keeping you in there long enough,

00:04:35.483 --> 00:04:37.735
but that's a different type of cold

00:04:37.735 --> 00:04:39.737
than what was being shown in "Archer."

00:04:39.737 --> 00:04:41.656
Like, that's Arctic-water cold.

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:51.207
Looked like he was putting a
mason glass explosive charge

00:04:51.207 --> 00:04:52.959
somewhere inside of the propeller.

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:55.253
OK. I mean, highly improbable.

00:04:55.253 --> 00:04:56.713
The propeller itself may not be

00:04:56.713 --> 00:04:59.716
the best place to actually put a charge.

00:04:59.716 --> 00:05:01.301
Actually, when we would do ship attack,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.052
combat sweeper operations,

00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:04.721
instead of actually the propeller itself,

00:05:04.721 --> 00:05:07.432
the underside of a vessel,
there's oftentimes struts

00:05:07.432 --> 00:05:09.600
and then the driveshaft that
goes out to the propeller.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.313
And we would actually put the
charges on the struts itself

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:15.648
to structurally damage the ship's ability

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:17.608
to work under its own power or navigate.

00:05:17.608 --> 00:05:19.319
If you blow up a propeller,

00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:22.405
and I'm not saying that this
is like a NASCAR swap-out fix,

00:05:22.405 --> 00:05:24.282
but you can put another propeller on.

00:05:24.282 --> 00:05:27.076
If you damage the structural
integrity of the ship

00:05:27.076 --> 00:05:30.705
and then the propeller
shaft is then broken

00:05:30.705 --> 00:05:32.415
and they have no ability to reattach that,

00:05:32.415 --> 00:05:34.834
again, it's a fixable
problem, but now we're talking

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:36.878
probably taking the boat out of the water,

00:05:36.878 --> 00:05:39.088
putting it into a dry dock,
and all of those things.

00:05:39.088 --> 00:05:41.799
So you can get more bang for your buck.

00:05:41.799 --> 00:05:43.343
Pun intended, I suppose.

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:48.473
How explosives are able
to work underwater?

00:05:48.473 --> 00:05:52.894
A lot of them are naturally
very resistant to the elements.

00:05:52.894 --> 00:05:54.562
C-4 is a good example.

00:05:54.562 --> 00:05:57.648
To get C-4 to explode, we
actually use blasting caps.

00:05:57.648 --> 00:05:59.609
So that functions underwater, though,

00:05:59.609 --> 00:06:02.570
just like it would in the air.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.739
Tough to rate a cartoon,
I think, for accuracy.

00:06:04.739 --> 00:06:06.366
This is going to have
to be on the lower end.

00:06:06.366 --> 00:06:08.284
I'm going to go with a 2.

00:06:08.284 --> 00:06:09.535
And the only reason he gets a 2

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:11.996
is I like the homemade mason charge

00:06:11.996 --> 00:06:15.166
that he somehow wedged into the propeller.

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:27.762
If you need to cross the
river, cross the river.

00:06:27.762 --> 00:06:29.722
You know, there's no
reason to actually pop up

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:33.226
in the middle of it and give
away your tactical advantage

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:35.061
if you are trying to sneak up on somebody.

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:37.522
You would either cross the
entire thing above the water

00:06:37.522 --> 00:06:40.024
or cross the entire thing below the water.

00:06:40.024 --> 00:06:43.319
Or go farther down or upstream

00:06:43.319 --> 00:06:45.655
and maybe find a log that's across it,

00:06:45.655 --> 00:06:46.739
so you don't have to get wet.

00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:48.366
'Cause being wet sucks.

00:06:54.997 --> 00:06:56.374
All right, stop right there.

00:06:56.374 --> 00:07:00.837
I have been asked about this
particular scene so many times,

00:07:00.837 --> 00:07:03.131
because people absolutely love it

00:07:03.131 --> 00:07:07.093
and they want to know how we
train to be able to do that.

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:08.594
And here's the answer:

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:09.637
we don't.

00:07:09.637 --> 00:07:12.181
Because, right, you would never
fall in any other direction.

00:07:12.181 --> 00:07:16.436
You just absolutely know
the exact place and time

00:07:16.436 --> 00:07:17.895
that somebody is going to fall

00:07:17.895 --> 00:07:19.147
when you shoot them in the head, right?

00:07:19.147 --> 00:07:21.274
Because why wouldn't you?
What could possibly go wrong?

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:23.651
But you've got to remember,
all of that was preceded by

00:07:23.651 --> 00:07:26.946
probably a 7.62 or a 0.300 Win Mag round

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:28.990
snapping through the jungle.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:30.575
The suppressor that's on the rifle,

00:07:30.575 --> 00:07:32.034
it's basically a muffler.

00:07:32.034 --> 00:07:33.953
And it contains some of the gas,

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:37.999
and it drastically reduces the
decibel output of the rifle.

00:07:37.999 --> 00:07:40.418
But bullets,

00:07:40.418 --> 00:07:42.420
when they fly through the air,

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.423
they're supersonic, so it makes a crack.

00:07:45.423 --> 00:07:48.176
And there is subsonic ammunition,

00:07:48.176 --> 00:07:49.969
and it's much quieter.

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:51.512
That's not what that person was shooting.

00:07:51.512 --> 00:07:53.764
The whole point of doing the charade

00:07:53.764 --> 00:07:56.601
of catching somebody and
lowering them into the water

00:07:56.601 --> 00:07:58.936
as if it was just eating
them into the abyss

00:07:58.936 --> 00:08:00.354
is 'cause you're trying to be sneaky.

00:08:00.354 --> 00:08:03.065
So going through all of
this in attempt to be sneaky

00:08:03.065 --> 00:08:06.194
and then cracking off
a round in the jungle,

00:08:06.194 --> 00:08:08.821
it defeats the entire purpose.

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:10.198
It's just, there's so,

00:08:10.198 --> 00:08:12.617
it's not how it works, people!

00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:22.335
Where you link up with a submarine

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:23.252
in the middle of the ocean,

00:08:23.252 --> 00:08:24.587
that's totally plausible.

00:08:24.587 --> 00:08:26.589
They were on a Zodiac,
which is going to have

00:08:26.589 --> 00:08:29.592
probably a 55- or a 35-horsepower engine.

00:08:29.592 --> 00:08:33.221
So they could easily go from
a landmass over the horizon

00:08:33.221 --> 00:08:35.515
out into international water,

00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:37.350
or really wherever the sub
would want to meet and do that.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:38.726
So that's totally legit.

00:08:47.902 --> 00:08:48.819
Yeah. Wow.

00:08:48.819 --> 00:08:50.821
So, they obviously had direct support

00:08:50.821 --> 00:08:53.407
from the United States
Navy to make this movie.

00:08:53.407 --> 00:08:56.619
And I'm actually surprised they
showed very much of the SDV,

00:08:56.619 --> 00:08:58.788
which stands for SEAL Delivery Vehicle.

00:08:58.788 --> 00:09:01.707
In essence, it is a miniature submarine.

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:05.503
It's smaller, harder to be discovered,

00:09:05.503 --> 00:09:07.880
and you can move people and equipment

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.675
a really far distance and then
go back to the submarine too,

00:09:10.675 --> 00:09:13.094
'cause you can go back and relink back up

00:09:13.094 --> 00:09:14.929
to that asset that you departed from.

00:09:14.929 --> 00:09:17.765
You are in your dive gear
until that dive is over,

00:09:17.765 --> 00:09:20.351
and it can be up to 14 hours.

00:09:20.351 --> 00:09:21.811
A lot of the times you're in dry suits.

00:09:21.811 --> 00:09:24.689
Dry and warm are two very different things

00:09:24.689 --> 00:09:26.607
that people should not confuse.

00:09:26.607 --> 00:09:28.442
They nailed it. It's very plausible

00:09:28.442 --> 00:09:31.153
that all of that would
occur on an SDV operation.

00:09:31.153 --> 00:09:32.488
I'm going to give them a 10 on that.

00:09:32.488 --> 00:09:35.283
The sniper water river catch is a zero.

00:09:35.283 --> 00:09:37.159
It actually might be a negative number.

00:09:44.083 --> 00:09:45.501
Is there a more obvious way

00:09:45.501 --> 00:09:47.378
to let somebody know that you're coming

00:09:47.378 --> 00:09:49.880
than white and orange
parachutes in the daytime,

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:51.716
jumping directly over the top of them?

00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:54.635
"Surprise! You didn't see that coming!"

00:09:54.635 --> 00:09:58.973
I've worn dive fins on my
feet for a free-fall jump

00:09:58.973 --> 00:10:01.017
and actually was falling
through the air with them on.

00:10:01.017 --> 00:10:04.562
More often than not, though,
the back strap of the fin,

00:10:04.562 --> 00:10:05.563
you put your feet through that,

00:10:05.563 --> 00:10:08.274
and you actually tape
the fin up to your leg.

00:10:08.274 --> 00:10:09.400
But if you're going to land in the water,

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:11.319
you're going to want to have fins on you

00:10:11.319 --> 00:10:13.112
in case you need to
have the ability to swim

00:10:13.112 --> 00:10:14.405
or exert yourself.

00:10:14.405 --> 00:10:15.990
You're going to need those fins.

00:10:20.161 --> 00:10:21.829
[laughs]

00:10:21.829 --> 00:10:23.164
Yes!

00:10:23.164 --> 00:10:24.373
And they had a fleet,

00:10:24.373 --> 00:10:28.002
a full fleet of submersibles
with spear guns.

00:10:28.002 --> 00:10:32.089
I am not aware of any engagement

00:10:32.089 --> 00:10:34.133
at any time in the history of human beings

00:10:34.133 --> 00:10:36.177
where anything like this has occurred.

00:10:36.177 --> 00:10:38.721
We are not trying to get
into fights in the water.

00:10:38.721 --> 00:10:39.847
Like, bottom line.

00:10:39.847 --> 00:10:42.516
We use it as a way to
disguise what we're doing

00:10:42.516 --> 00:10:44.185
or to get from A to B

00:10:44.185 --> 00:10:45.561
or to be sneaky, right?

00:10:45.561 --> 00:10:46.896
Like, "Well, I don't
know where they went."

00:10:46.896 --> 00:10:48.439
Like, "Bloop, here we
are! We're in the water.

00:10:48.439 --> 00:10:49.899
You didn't see us, surprise!"

00:10:49.899 --> 00:10:52.568
We're not looking to
get into a fight, ever.

00:10:52.568 --> 00:10:53.611
So, no.

00:10:53.611 --> 00:10:55.279
We're not bringing tridents or spears

00:10:55.279 --> 00:10:56.530
or any of that stuff.

00:11:02.244 --> 00:11:05.164
I like how they're cutting the hoses.

00:11:05.164 --> 00:11:08.626
Unfortunately, there is
usually an inhalation hose

00:11:08.626 --> 00:11:10.336
and an exhalation hose,

00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:11.712
and they probably could still breathe

00:11:11.712 --> 00:11:12.880
if they cut the wrong one.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:15.007
We always had dive knives on us,

00:11:15.007 --> 00:11:17.426
but it's for cutting gear
if something gets hung up.

00:11:17.426 --> 00:11:20.554
There was never a
thought in my mind, ever,

00:11:20.554 --> 00:11:23.432
of pulling that out with the
intent of fighting with it.

00:11:23.432 --> 00:11:25.142
It was for anything but that.

00:11:25.142 --> 00:11:28.145
Like, Phillips-head screws,
using the tip of your knife,

00:11:28.145 --> 00:11:30.523
like, that's what we use dive knives for.

00:11:35.236 --> 00:11:37.655
I like the idea of what
he's got in his mouth.

00:11:37.655 --> 00:11:40.991
I'll call that the gen-one Spare Air.

00:11:40.991 --> 00:11:42.576
Which, with a canister of that size,

00:11:42.576 --> 00:11:45.329
I feel like you have about
one-half of one breath.

00:11:45.329 --> 00:11:47.790
So he certainly wouldn't
be able to stay underwater.

00:11:54.964 --> 00:11:57.466
And then the concussive
device in the water.

00:11:57.466 --> 00:12:00.928
Concussion in the way that
shock waves travel underwater

00:12:00.928 --> 00:12:02.847
is a very, very real thing.

00:12:02.847 --> 00:12:05.474
Let's just say that was
a grenade of some kind.

00:12:05.474 --> 00:12:06.642
That would probably kill the people

00:12:06.642 --> 00:12:08.686
inside of that contained area,

00:12:08.686 --> 00:12:10.688
but it probably would
damage him a little bit too.

00:12:10.688 --> 00:12:12.732
Especially if there's open
windows and portholes,

00:12:12.732 --> 00:12:14.817
'cause that stuff will bounce out as well.

00:12:14.817 --> 00:12:17.111
So it's plausible,

00:12:17.111 --> 00:12:18.487
but I would say you would
want to get the [beep]

00:12:18.487 --> 00:12:19.989
out of there before that thing went off.

00:12:19.989 --> 00:12:23.325
Like, out of the water
before that thing went off.

00:12:23.325 --> 00:12:25.911
I don't even know if I can give
this one a technical review.

00:12:33.502 --> 00:12:35.713
So, I didn't get a good look at the gun

00:12:35.713 --> 00:12:37.882
that he shot that out
of. My guess would be

00:12:37.882 --> 00:12:41.177
probably some version of
the M203 grenade launcher.

00:12:41.177 --> 00:12:44.180
The projectile doesn't have this

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:46.640
continuous propellant,

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:48.684
like it did in that, and
it just pushes along.

00:12:48.684 --> 00:12:51.270
And people, I'm sure, want to know

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.439
if you can fire guns underwater.

00:12:53.439 --> 00:12:56.692
You can fire a gun generally one time.

00:12:56.692 --> 00:12:59.361
They're not designed to
function in an environment

00:12:59.361 --> 00:13:02.114
that has that much
feedback from the water.

00:13:02.114 --> 00:13:06.202
You could probably shoot a 203 underwater,

00:13:06.202 --> 00:13:07.578
but it's not going to look like that.

00:13:07.578 --> 00:13:09.622
It would probably come out of the barrel,

00:13:09.622 --> 00:13:11.540
if it even cleared the barrel,

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:13.876
and then just drop right off.

00:13:13.876 --> 00:13:16.086
And they also arm by spinning.

00:13:16.086 --> 00:13:17.630
That's why it has a
minimum arming distance,

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:18.839
usually of 33 yards.

00:13:18.839 --> 00:13:23.052
So there's a lot of things
that were completely impossible

00:13:23.052 --> 00:13:25.137
in that particular scene right there.

00:13:31.852 --> 00:13:33.521
If you just look at how inefficient

00:13:33.521 --> 00:13:34.647
the swimming motion was.

00:13:34.647 --> 00:13:36.607
What kills a lot of your energy,

00:13:36.607 --> 00:13:37.441
there's two things.

00:13:37.441 --> 00:13:38.901
One, they had a weapon slung around them

00:13:38.901 --> 00:13:40.402
that was very loose,

00:13:40.402 --> 00:13:41.320
and their shoes.

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:43.823
It is so hard to swim with shoes.

00:13:43.823 --> 00:13:45.449
I think that video highlighted

00:13:45.449 --> 00:13:47.827
the difference between fins and no fins.

00:13:47.827 --> 00:13:48.994
That's what swimming looks like

00:13:48.994 --> 00:13:50.621
when you don't have fins
on and you have shoes.

00:13:50.621 --> 00:13:52.248
That's why you want to
have fins on top of that.

00:13:52.248 --> 00:13:53.290
If we were jumping and there wasn't

00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:54.792
going to be that much swimming,

00:13:54.792 --> 00:13:57.169
I'd go with one that
had maximum flexibility,

00:13:57.169 --> 00:13:58.587
so I could tape it up and wear the thing

00:13:58.587 --> 00:13:59.922
as I was leaving the aircraft.

00:13:59.922 --> 00:14:01.549
If I had to wear what they had on,

00:14:01.549 --> 00:14:03.050
I'd be pulling myself along the floor.

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:04.718
I think it would be easier,
and it's very positive,

00:14:04.718 --> 00:14:08.264
and you'd get more
mileage for your effort.

00:14:15.229 --> 00:14:18.023
That's why you need a
knife in that situation.

00:14:18.023 --> 00:14:19.316
But they weren't diving,

00:14:19.316 --> 00:14:20.943
so they didn't have their
dive knives with them.

00:14:20.943 --> 00:14:23.195
However, they had a bunch of guns.

00:14:23.195 --> 00:14:25.114
I mean, they didn't spend
5 bucks on a pocketknife?

00:14:25.114 --> 00:14:26.866
I mean, what's going on here?

00:14:26.866 --> 00:14:28.492
I'm going to give them a 3 at best.

00:14:28.492 --> 00:14:30.327
It's a stretch. They're reaching for a 3,

00:14:30.327 --> 00:14:31.912
but I'll give them a 3.

00:14:31.912 --> 00:14:35.457
I found door No. 3. And I'm taking it.

00:14:43.883 --> 00:14:45.676
Oh, that's a national treasure, all right.

00:14:45.676 --> 00:14:46.802
You know, jumping off of a,

00:14:46.802 --> 00:14:48.137
I think it was the aircraft carrier,

00:14:48.137 --> 00:14:51.223
that's probably going to be 50, 60 feet.

00:14:51.223 --> 00:14:53.309
I'm here for that. That's
definitely survivable.

00:14:53.309 --> 00:14:54.810
You could definitely do that.

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:03.569
So, we navigated with,

00:15:03.569 --> 00:15:05.070
we call them nav boards,

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:07.907
and we had three really
exciting items on there

00:15:07.907 --> 00:15:10.618
that we used to get to stare
at for hours at a time.

00:15:10.618 --> 00:15:13.746
The first one you already
mentioned. Was a compass.

00:15:13.746 --> 00:15:16.790
The next entertaining
tool was a depth gauge,

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:19.752
and, lastly, a stopwatch.

00:15:19.752 --> 00:15:20.794
That was it.

00:15:20.794 --> 00:15:23.839
We dive in pairs, but only one person

00:15:23.839 --> 00:15:25.090
gets the navigation board.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:26.926
So somebody else out there is just like,

00:15:26.926 --> 00:15:30.012
"I hate my life. This
dive is taking forever.

00:15:30.012 --> 00:15:32.014
I don't even have anything to look at."

00:15:32.014 --> 00:15:33.599
So sometimes when I was the buddy,

00:15:33.599 --> 00:15:35.935
I would just swim on top of my dive buddy

00:15:35.935 --> 00:15:37.603
and just look at the navigation board too

00:15:37.603 --> 00:15:38.938
because I was so bored.

00:15:38.938 --> 00:15:40.648
I just wanted to look at numbers

00:15:40.648 --> 00:15:43.108
slowly just, like, creeping around.

00:15:46.403 --> 00:15:47.905
Sadusky: Can you see Gates in the water?

00:15:47.905 --> 00:15:50.699
Sir, it's the Hudson. Nothing is visible.

00:15:50.699 --> 00:15:53.410
So, all these people that
are above them looking down

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:54.620
are going to see the bubbles.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:56.455
This would be an example
of where closed circuit

00:15:56.455 --> 00:15:57.873
would be better than open circuit.

00:15:57.873 --> 00:16:00.334
Because even if that guy
grabbed him right underwater,

00:16:00.334 --> 00:16:02.628
every time they exhale,
the bubbles are coming up.

00:16:02.628 --> 00:16:05.881
It's actually really easy
to track divers' progress

00:16:05.881 --> 00:16:08.509
from above when they're
breathing open circuit.

00:16:08.509 --> 00:16:09.802
'Cause open circuit you
can see the bubbles,

00:16:09.802 --> 00:16:12.388
closed circuit you can be sneaky.

00:16:12.388 --> 00:16:14.223
So it's just a closed-loop system

00:16:14.223 --> 00:16:16.934
that only allows the gas to
flow through in one direction,

00:16:16.934 --> 00:16:17.851
because obviously you wouldn't want it

00:16:17.851 --> 00:16:19.269
coming back and forth.

00:16:19.269 --> 00:16:20.688
And you push it through
a chemical scrubber,

00:16:20.688 --> 00:16:22.940
it pulls out the carbon
dioxide, and, boom,

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:24.066
you're off and running.

00:16:24.066 --> 00:16:27.236
There's a 100% chance that
you could actually do that.

00:16:27.236 --> 00:16:30.447
But if you wanted to do it
and actually not be seen,

00:16:30.447 --> 00:16:32.282
I'd say closed circuit is a better option.

00:16:32.282 --> 00:16:34.201
I'll give him a

00:16:34.201 --> 00:16:34.952
7.

00:16:37.454 --> 00:16:39.039
Bond: Conserve your gas supply.

00:16:39.039 --> 00:16:41.542
Speak only when necessary.

00:16:41.542 --> 00:16:42.918
Going to have to be a radio.

00:16:42.918 --> 00:16:45.337
There's no way it's going to
be a Bluetooth connection.

00:16:45.337 --> 00:16:47.840
I have no experience with doing that.

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:49.633
There's not really anybody
that you would be talking to,

00:16:49.633 --> 00:16:52.136
nor do I know what you would
necessarily talk about.

00:16:52.136 --> 00:16:55.389
The diving that we did
was hand-signal-based.

00:16:55.389 --> 00:16:58.726
Or you'd have a grease pencil
and a piece of plastic,

00:16:58.726 --> 00:17:00.227
and you could write on it.

00:17:00.227 --> 00:17:02.354
Other than that, it was squeezes

00:17:02.354 --> 00:17:03.605
or just [blabbing].

00:17:03.605 --> 00:17:06.400
And it's amazing actually how much

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.193
you can learn what the
other person is saying.

00:17:08.193 --> 00:17:10.487
You're almost actually
holding a conversation.

00:17:10.487 --> 00:17:11.822
So, very low speed.

00:17:11.822 --> 00:17:13.157
Even if something went wrong,

00:17:13.157 --> 00:17:14.867
the No. 1 thing you do
is you stop the dive

00:17:14.867 --> 00:17:15.701
and you go to the surface,

00:17:15.701 --> 00:17:17.202
'cause you can take the stuff
out of your mouth and be like,

00:17:17.202 --> 00:17:18.412
"Hey, man, what's going on?"

00:17:18.412 --> 00:17:19.705
And you can restart the dive.

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:28.714
Yeah, that's real. Sharks
like to go in stuff too.

00:17:28.714 --> 00:17:30.758
When things would bump into me,

00:17:30.758 --> 00:17:32.468
the theory that I went with

00:17:32.468 --> 00:17:35.846
was if I don't look, it didn't happen.

00:17:35.846 --> 00:17:38.307
So we would be swimming around
a lot of times at night,

00:17:38.307 --> 00:17:40.059
and then I would get slammed,

00:17:40.059 --> 00:17:42.227
and my whole thought process was,

00:17:42.227 --> 00:17:44.772
"Nope, I'm not gonna look at that.

00:17:44.772 --> 00:17:46.398
I'm gonna pretend that it didn't happen."

00:17:46.398 --> 00:17:48.025
It probably was, like, a sea lion.

00:17:48.025 --> 00:17:49.860
You know, it could have been a dolphin.

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:50.778
It might've been a shark.

00:17:50.778 --> 00:17:52.529
I don't know. I didn't look.

00:17:52.529 --> 00:17:56.200
I would say that everything that they did

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:57.576
is possible.

00:17:57.576 --> 00:18:00.204
They may not have had all
the right equipment to do it,

00:18:00.204 --> 00:18:01.747
but I'll give it a 7.

00:18:09.171 --> 00:18:11.590
So, it requires different equipment.

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:15.803
Oftentimes, even to get
into these cave systems,

00:18:15.803 --> 00:18:20.682
it's a very high-risk,
high-consequence activity.

00:18:20.682 --> 00:18:21.850
Hard pass.

00:18:23.727 --> 00:18:26.105
Stay calm! We're going to buddy breathe.

00:18:29.441 --> 00:18:31.068
They decided that they were
going to buddy breathe,

00:18:31.068 --> 00:18:32.903
which is just basically
sharing one apparatus

00:18:32.903 --> 00:18:35.239
between two people.

00:18:35.239 --> 00:18:36.406
If you're going to do that,

00:18:36.406 --> 00:18:38.033
you're going to have to do
more than just sit there

00:18:38.033 --> 00:18:40.953
and buddy breathe until
your oxygen or air is gone.

00:18:40.953 --> 00:18:41.829
Because you notice they were staying

00:18:41.829 --> 00:18:43.330
in exactly the same spot.

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:45.124
So you could just sit there and do that

00:18:45.124 --> 00:18:47.126
and then run out of air and die together,

00:18:47.126 --> 00:18:50.337
or maybe buddy breathe
and start making your way

00:18:50.337 --> 00:18:52.422
towards how you can get
the [beep] out of there.

00:19:01.557 --> 00:19:03.433
Buddy breathing

00:19:03.433 --> 00:19:06.603
can kind of go exactly
like it went in this movie.

00:19:06.603 --> 00:19:09.356
People's ability to hold their breath

00:19:09.356 --> 00:19:11.024
varies greatly.

00:19:11.024 --> 00:19:13.026
And when the regulator is in your mouth

00:19:13.026 --> 00:19:15.154
or the mask is on your
face and you're breathing,

00:19:15.154 --> 00:19:17.030
you feel a lot better

00:19:17.030 --> 00:19:18.949
than the person who is
holding their breath,

00:19:18.949 --> 00:19:19.908
watching you breathe,

00:19:19.908 --> 00:19:21.243
wishing that they were breathing,

00:19:21.243 --> 00:19:23.453
wanting the mask that you're wearing.

00:19:23.453 --> 00:19:25.664
So it can become a little bit of a battle,

00:19:25.664 --> 00:19:27.124
but you have to work together.

00:19:27.124 --> 00:19:30.502
You follow the procedure,
regardless if you're scared,

00:19:30.502 --> 00:19:32.546
cold, wet, hungry, tired,

00:19:32.546 --> 00:19:34.256
fill in the blank. Or
everything in between.

00:19:34.256 --> 00:19:36.091
People can get very emotionally involved

00:19:36.091 --> 00:19:38.218
in those situations, where
what they really need to do

00:19:38.218 --> 00:19:39.845
is procedurally work through it,

00:19:39.845 --> 00:19:42.598
as opposed to allowing
their emotions to take over.

00:19:42.598 --> 00:19:44.224
And you have to work as a team.

00:19:44.224 --> 00:19:46.059
Yeah. It would be one of
the worst-case scenarios

00:19:46.059 --> 00:19:48.812
to have something happen like that.

00:19:48.812 --> 00:19:50.772
Not the way that I would
want to go out, for sure.

00:19:50.772 --> 00:19:52.274
I'm going to give that a 7.

00:19:52.274 --> 00:19:54.109
So, if you liked this video
and you want to see more,

00:19:54.109 --> 00:19:56.862
do me a favor and click the
link that's above my head.

